# tools/font5x7_genpunct_witness.rish -- the open general-punctuation corpus proves itself on metal (Season G, font content).
#
# Season G (Open Media Primitives) - more content BEYOND printable ASCII for the open glyph atlas image/glyph.rye
# (gfa1), a sibling of the ASCII punctuation witness (tools/font5x7_punct_witness.rish, the thirteen straight marks).
#
# The crux this witness proves is a REAL, OPEN, hand-drawn general-punctuation corpus: the seven "smart" marks real
# rendered text carries above ASCII -- the en dash and em dash (U+2013 U+2014), the curly single and double quotation
# marks (U+2018 U+2019 U+201C U+201D), and the horizontal ellipsis (U+2026), each a 5x7 coverage grid drawn in the open
# as ASCII line art in image/font5x7_genpunct.rye, built into a gfa1 atlas and proven on metal. Our own prose stays
# plain ASCII by the ascii-first rule, yet a surface that paints a document or a quoted foreign name must be able to
# SHOW these. A full round-trip recovers every codepoint, advance, and coverage byte byte-for-byte; all seven look up
# present and carry the shared advance while an ASCII hyphen misses; no two share coverage (opening and closing quotes
# really differ); and three structural bonds hold -- the en dash is a strict subset of the em dash, each double quote
# out-inks its single twin, and the ellipsis is exactly three evenly spaced baseline dots. Compression is reported as
# a fact, never asserted. No network, no key, no funds -- the marks a surface reads the day it paints real text.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/font5x7_genpunct_witness.rish

say "font5x7-genpunct: Language EN -- Season G font content beyond ASCII; the seven smart-punctuation marks (en/em dashes, curly single and double quotes, ellipsis) drawn in the open as ASCII line art, built into the gfa1 atlas, so a surface can paint real rendered text that carries them."
say "font5x7-genpunct: Style Radiant -- the drawings are the specification and the bytes are derived from them, so decode(encode(atlas)) recovers every mark byte for byte; our own hand, permissively ours, no external font to license and no web-search gate."
say "font5x7-genpunct: Lens TAME -- u32 in-memory counts, u8 coverage bytes; every buffer names a maximum (cell_area - genpunct_count - the atlas ceilings it rests on); each function states its invariants positively; codepoints explicit and strictly ascending; the source stays plain ASCII with numeric codepoints though the glyphs cover a non-ASCII range; the en-subset-of-em, double-out-inks-single, and three-baseline-dots properties each proven; compression a reported fact never a false invariant; no network, no key, no funds."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "image/bin/font5x7_genpunct"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p image/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build image/font5x7_genpunct.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "font5x7-genpunct: build failed -- ${build.err}"

let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "font5x7-genpunct: selftest exited non-zero -- ${selftest.out}"

let out = trim selftest.out
say "font5x7-genpunct: ${out}"

assert selftest.out contains "GREEN image-font5x7-genpunct" else "font5x7-genpunct: selftest did not print the GREEN corpus line"

assert selftest.out contains "round-trip byte-identical" else "font5x7-genpunct: round-trip was not byte-identical"

assert selftest.out contains "en subset of em" else "font5x7-genpunct: the structural bonds were not reported"

say "font5x7-genpunct: GREEN -- the open general-punctuation corpus whole; seven smart marks round-trip byte-identical, all present, distinct, legible, and structurally bonded; a surface can paint real text that carries them."
